A||||19||fierocritter||more horsepower out of your Fiero||||5||
Z||000000||fierocritter||11-13-2001||12:34 AM||||I have an 85 Fiero Gt V-6, 4-speed manual, 145,000 miles on it. Top end of motor rebuilt at 142,000. The only reason for rebuild was i snapped the spark plugs off in the block. Original factory plugs. Rebuilt the heads when I had them off, new seals, gaskets. Pistons where surprisingly clean, minor carbon. It has 80+ psi off oil pressure when starting and at full operating temp. it goes down to about 60 psi. Have bosch platinum 4"s installed, new wires, new coil, and module. What other ideas are out there for increasing horse? I have the timing advanced to 12 degrees. I don't have alot of money, but sometimes spending a little can do alot. I need some tricks of the trade. Will getting rid of the cat increase horsepower, and how much. Example: cutting the post on a mass air flow housing on a mustang gt will give an increase in horse. Is there any tricks for the Fiero? any info greatly appreaciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||11-13-2001||01:42 AM||||1. block off t-body coolant lines<BR>2. block off EGR <BR>3. advance timing<BR>4. cut weight<P>All of these will give you some extra ummp but 2 and 3 may make it necassary to run premium fuel because of knock.<BR>The best thing is that these suggestions won't cost you anything if you're handy.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phil||11-13-2001||07:01 AM||||Did you grind out the restrictions in the exhaust manifolds while you had them off? 6-8 HP right there - free too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierocritter||11-13-2001||09:36 AM||||What does cutting the coolant lines do to increase your horsepower? No Egr valve will also increase? I did not know about grinding something out of the manifold, what do I grind out? Please help! Need Power||||reg||1
Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||11-13-2001||09:49 AM||||If you look inside the manifold, you'll know instantly what needs to be ground out.  When GM made the manifolds, they cut very small holes in the "collector" coming off each cylindar... there is usually 1/4"-1/2" of extra metal that can be easily ground off.<P>Other easy performance things... K&N filter ($20-$30), Bored TB & Intake Manifold (approx $200?- Darrel Morse), MSD Ignition ($200?), Undersize pulleys ($80 FieroStore)... that's just a few more offhand.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierocritter||11-13-2001||10:10 AM||||cutting the coolant lines to the throttle boby will increase my horsepower? What about cutting the cat out of my exhaust system?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jefrysuko||11-13-2001||10:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierocritter:<BR><B>cutting the coolant lines to the throttle body will increase my horsepower? What about cutting the cat out of my exhaust system?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Some people just block off the coolant lines with some type of valve others just take them off all together.  The idea here is that you don't want anything on the intake side any hotter than it has to be because it will heat up the air.  The cooler the air is the denser it will be and the more that can be crammed into the combustion chamber.<P>Yes either a high flow cat or eliminating it all together will give you a little horsepower.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierocritter||11-13-2001||10:34 AM||||the cat is newer privious owner replaced it before sale. Would I be able to feel the increase if i removed the cat and put a section of pipe instead? I understand about the coolant lines. Do you think my car would be louder without the cat?||||reg||1
